  "slug": "design-principle-hypometabolism-df23",
  "summary": "Recent single-cell benchmarks test whether evaluation protocols and ground-truth labels, not model architecture, determine reported performance.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these three preprints is that reported performance in single-cell and spatial proteomics depends on how ground truth is defined and how evaluation is structured. One preprint finds that LLM annotation of mass cytometry clusters reaches 85-98% metacluster concordance, within the range where human experts disagree with each other, so agreement with annotation is bounded by expert variability. Another shows that random cell splits inflate single-cell classifier F1 relative to donor-held-out evaluation, with the size of the bias depending on the classification regime. A third benchmarks learning-based and alignment-based methods for cross-slice protein imputation in spatial single-cell proteomics, a comparison of methods rather than of evaluation protocol. The new contribution is explicit quantification of how split design and reference labels shift conclusions. Not all items address validity directly.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-29"
}
